__d(function(e,a,i,t,n,o,r){n.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Science",textbook:"Science \u2014 Textbook for Class VI",chapter_number:6,chapter_title:"Changes Around Us",total_cards:20,cards:[{id:"fc01",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a reversible change? Give two examples.",back:"A reversible change is one in which the original substance can be recovered \u2014 the change can be undone. Examples: (1) Melting of ice \u2014 cooled water becomes ice again. (2) Dissolving sugar in water \u2014 evaporate the water and get sugar back. Other examples: stretching a rubber band, folding paper, melting wax.",memory_hook:"REVERSIBLE = can REVERSE. Think of a video you can rewind and play again \u2014 the original state is restored."},{id:"fc02",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is an irreversible change? Give two examples.",back:"An irreversible change is one in which the original substance CANNOT be recovered \u2014 the change cannot be undone, and a new substance is usually formed. Examples: (1) Burning of paper \u2014 ash and smoke formed; cannot get paper back. (2) Cooking an egg \u2014 raw proteins permanently changed; cannot become raw again. Other examples: souring of milk, rusting of iron, setting of cement.",memory_hook:"IRREVERSIBLE = IR = It's Really final. Burns, cooks, rusts, rots = done forever."},{id:"fc03",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the relationship between physical changes and reversibility?",back:"Physical changes usually involve changes in shape, size, or state of a substance \u2014 the chemical composition remains the same. Most physical changes are reversible. Examples: melting (state change \u2014 reversible), dissolving (physical dispersal \u2014 reversible), cutting (shape change \u2014 can be re-joined). Chemical changes, on the other hand, usually produce new substances and are irreversible.",memory_hook:"Physical = form change only = usually reversible. Chemical = NEW substance = usually irreversible."},{id:"fc04",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Classify each change as reversible or irreversible: (a) Melting of butter (b) Burning of wood (c) Stretching a rubber band (d) Souring of milk",back:"(a) Melting of butter \u2014 REVERSIBLE. Cool the melted butter and it solidifies again. (b) Burning of wood \u2014 IRREVERSIBLE. Ash and gases form; wood cannot be recovered. (c) Stretching a rubber band \u2014 REVERSIBLE. Release it and it returns to original shape. (d) Souring of milk \u2014 IRREVERSIBLE. Bacteria chemically change milk to curd; fresh milk cannot be recovered.",memory_hook:"Test: Can you get the original back? YES = reversible. NO = irreversible. Apply this test to everything."},{id:"fc05",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Is dissolving sugar in water reversible or irreversible? How do you know?",back:"Reversible. When sugar dissolves in water, the sugar disperses into tiny particles but is NOT chemically changed. You can recover the sugar by evaporating the water \u2014 the sugar crystals will reappear. This is proof that dissolution is a physical (reversible) change. The sugar was always there; it was just invisible while dissolved.",memory_hook:"Dissolving = hiding, not dying. Sugar hides in water but comes back when water evaporates. It's reversible."},{id:"fc06",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why is cooking an egg an irreversible change?",back:"When an egg is cooked, the proteins in the egg white and yolk undergo denaturation \u2014 their molecular structure permanently changes due to heat. The raw, transparent egg white becomes an opaque, rubbery solid. This is a chemical change \u2014 new substances with different properties are formed. No matter how much you cool the cooked egg, it cannot become raw again.",memory_hook:"Once an egg is cooked, it's DONE \u2014 cannot unscramble scrambled eggs! Cooking = chemical change = irreversible."},{id:"fc07",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What happens to most substances when they are heated? What is this called?",back:"Most substances expand (increase in size/volume) when heated. This is called thermal expansion. The particles of the substance vibrate more vigorously when heated and push apart, needing more space. When the substance is cooled, the particles slow down and come closer \u2014 the substance contracts (decreases in size). Expansion and contraction due to temperature change are almost always reversible.",memory_hook:"HEAT makes things Hyper and spread out (Expansion). COLD makes things Calm and Compact (Contraction)."},{id:"fc08",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why do railway tracks have small gaps at the joints between rail sections?",back:"Railway tracks are made of metal (steel), which expands when heated in summer. If the rails were laid end-to-end without gaps, the expanding metal would have nowhere to go and would push against adjacent rails, causing them to buckle (bend). The small gaps at joints (expansion joints) provide space for the metal to expand safely without damaging the track. In winter, the rails contract and the gaps help prevent cracking.",memory_hook:"Rail gaps = breathing room for metal. Heat \u2192 expand \u2192 needs space. Gap provides that space. Without gaps = buckled tracks = derailment risk."},{id:"fc09",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"A glass jar has a tight metal lid that is hard to open. Your parent runs hot water over the lid for 30 seconds, and it opens easily. Why?",back:"Metal expands more than glass when heated. When hot water is run over the lid, the metal lid expands slightly \u2014 this expansion loosens the tight seal between the metal lid and the glass jar. The expanded lid now has a slightly larger circumference and is easier to turn and remove. This is a practical everyday application of thermal expansion.",memory_hook:"Hot water + metal lid = lid expands = loosens = opens. Thermal expansion solves the problem."},{id:"fc10",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Name five examples of irreversible changes from everyday life.",back:"(1) Burning of paper or wood \u2014 ash and smoke formed. (2) Cooking food (frying, boiling) \u2014 new substances formed. (3) Souring of milk into curd \u2014 bacterial chemical change. (4) Rusting of iron \u2014 iron oxide (rust) formed. (5) Setting of cement \u2014 hardens permanently into concrete. All involve new substances being formed that cannot revert to the original.",memory_hook:"BCRRS = Burn, Cook, Rust, Rot, Set-cement. All irreversible. BCRRS \u2014 five final changes."},{id:"fc11",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Name five examples of reversible changes from everyday life.",back:"(1) Melting of ice \u2014 water can be frozen again. (2) Dissolving sugar in water \u2014 evaporate to recover sugar. (3) Stretching a rubber band \u2014 returns to original shape. (4) Folding paper \u2014 can be unfolded. (5) Melting wax \u2014 solidifies when cooled. All are physical changes where the original substance can be recovered.",memory_hook:"MDSF M = Melt, Dissolve, Stretch, Fold, Melt-wax. All 'undo-able'. Physical changes, physical undoing."},{id:"fc12",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How does a thermometer work? Which scientific principle does it use?",back:"A thermometer contains liquid mercury or coloured alcohol in a narrow glass tube. When temperature rises, the liquid expands and rises up the tube. When temperature falls, the liquid contracts and drops in the tube. The height of the liquid column indicates the temperature. This works because of thermal expansion \u2014 liquids expand measurably with temperature, and this expansion is proportional to the temperature rise.",memory_hook:"Thermometer = liquid expansion meter. More heat \u2192 liquid expands \u2192 climbs higher. The expanding liquid is the measuring tool."},{id:"fc13",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Is rusting of iron a reversible or irreversible change? Explain why.",back:"Irreversible. When iron rusts, it combines with oxygen and moisture in the air to form iron oxide (rust) \u2014 a new compound with completely different properties. Rust is reddish-brown, brittle, and flaky \u2014 very different from shiny, strong iron. This is a chemical change. You cannot take rust and easily convert it back to iron in everyday conditions.",memory_hook:"Rust = iron + oxygen = new substance. New substance = irreversible. Rust is born, iron is gone."},{id:"fc14",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Water is unusual in expansion behaviour. How is it different from most substances?",back:"Most substances expand when heated and contract when cooled. Water mostly follows this rule, BUT water is unusual when it freezes \u2014 it EXPANDS upon freezing (going from liquid to solid). This is why ice floats on water (ice is less dense than liquid water). Most other liquids contract when they freeze (become denser). This unique property of water has important implications for aquatic life in cold climates.",memory_hook:"Water is the REBEL of expansion. Most solids are denser than their liquid, but ice (solid water) FLOATS on liquid water \u2014 water expanded when it froze."},{id:"fc15",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Your dosa batter is poured on a hot tawa and becomes a crispy dosa. Is this reversible or irreversible? Why?",back:"Irreversible. Cooking the dosa batter on the hot tawa involves chemical changes \u2014 the proteins in the batter denature (permanently change structure), the starches cook and change texture, and the surface becomes crispy through complex chemical reactions (Maillard browning). The dosa cannot be converted back to liquid batter. Cooking always involves irreversible chemical changes.",memory_hook:"Can a dosa become batter again? NO. Cooking = irreversible chemical change every time. Once cooked, always cooked."},{id:"fc16",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Classify: (a) Setting of cement (b) Folding a cloth (c) Burning a candle (d) Inflating a balloon",back:"(a) Setting of cement \u2014 IRREVERSIBLE. Wet cement undergoes a chemical reaction and permanently hardens. (b) Folding a cloth \u2014 REVERSIBLE. The cloth can be unfolded; no new substance formed. (c) Burning a candle \u2014 IRREVERSIBLE. Wax is burned and converted to water vapour and CO\u2082; you cannot get the wax back from the gases. (d) Inflating a balloon \u2014 REVERSIBLE. The balloon can be deflated; the air and rubber return to original state.",memory_hook:"Cement sets forever. Candle burns away. Cloth folds back. Balloon deflates back. Two irreversible, two reversible."},{id:"fc17",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Electrical wires between poles hang loosely with a sag. Why are they not pulled tightly?",back:"Metals contract when cooled. In winter, the metal wires contract and become shorter. If wires were pulled tightly in summer (when they are longer due to expansion), they would contract in winter and could snap (break) due to the tension. By allowing the wires to hang loosely (with sag), there is enough extra length to accommodate winter contraction without breaking. This is a safety design using the principle of thermal contraction.",memory_hook:"Sagging wires = planned for winter. They sag in summer (longer) and become taut-but-safe in winter (shorter). No sag = snap in winter."},{id:"fc18",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Karnataka is famous for Bidri craft from Bidar. The process involves casting zinc-copper alloy and then treating it. Is this an example of reversible or irreversible change?",back:"Irreversible. Bidriware involves melting and casting metal alloys (a combination of physical and chemical changes in alloying), then blackening the surface using special Bidar soil. Once the metal solidifies into the decorative shape and the surface treatment is applied, the process cannot be reversed. The alloy composition and surface treatment create a new form of the material. This is irreversible craft transformation.",memory_hook:"Craft + Chemistry = Irreversible art. Bidri's beauty is permanent \u2014 that's the point of making it."},{id:"fc19",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Can a change be partly reversible and partly irreversible? Give an example.",back:"Yes. Burning a candle is an example: (1) The melting of wax near the flame is REVERSIBLE \u2014 molten wax dripping down the candle can solidify again. (2) The burning/combustion of wax vapour is IRREVERSIBLE \u2014 the wax vapour combusts to form water vapour and carbon dioxide, which cannot become wax again. So the same process (burning candle) involves both a reversible physical change (melting) and an irreversible chemical change (combustion).",memory_hook:"Candle = two changes in one. Melting (reversible) + Burning (irreversible). Look for both kinds in complex changes."},{id:"fc20",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Is the making of curd (dahi) from milk reversible or irreversible? Explain with science.",back:"Irreversible. When milk is cultured to make curd (dahi), bacteria (Lactobacillus) convert lactose (milk sugar) into lactic acid through fermentation. This lactic acid causes the milk proteins (casein) to coagulate and set into curd. This is a biochemical (chemical) change \u2014 new substances are formed (lactic acid, coagulated proteins). You cannot convert curd back into fresh liquid milk.",memory_hook:"Milk \u2192 curd = one-way journey. Bacteria open a chemical door that cannot be closed. No uncurding possible."}]}},2901,[]);